[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Comprehensive Testing in .NET: Unit and Integration Testing"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Robust software engineering requires a comprehensive testing strategy. In the .NET ecosystem, modern testing relies on a powerful stack of tools: xUnit for test execution, Moq for mocking dependencies, FluentAssertions for expressive assertions, and Testcontainers for spinning up real infrastructure dependencies inside Docker containers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Setting Up the Testing Stack"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To begin, create a new xUnit test project and install the necessary NuGet packages via the .NET CLI."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"dotnet new xunit -n MyProject.Tests
cd MyProject.Tests
dotnet add package Moq
dotnet add package FluentAssertions
dotnet add package Testcontainers.PostgreSql
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Writing Unit Tests with xUnit, Moq, and FluentAssertions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unit tests isolate a single unit of code, typically a class or method, by mocking out external dependencies like databases or HTTP clients. Below is an example of testing an OrderService that relies on an IEmailService and an IOrderRepository."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"using FluentAssertions;
using Moq;
using Xunit;

public class OrderServiceTests
{
    [Fact]
    public async Task CompleteOrderAsync_ShouldSendEmailAndSave_WhenOrderIsValid()
    {
        // Arrange
        var orderRepoMock = new Mock<IOrderRepository>();
        var emailServiceMock = new Mock<IEmailService>();

        var service = new OrderService(orderRepoMock.Object, emailServiceMock.Object);
        var orderId = 42;

        // Act
        await service.CompleteOrderAsync(orderId);

        // Assert
        orderRepoMock.Verify(r => r.UpdateStatusAsync(orderId, OrderStatus.Completed), Times.Once);
        emailServiceMock.Verify(e => e.SendConfirmationEmailAsync(orderId), Times.Once);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Writing Integration Tests with Testcontainers"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"While unit tests are fast, integration tests verify that multiple components work together, including real data stores. Testcontainers allows you to spin up lightweight, throwaway instances of databases like PostgreSQL in Docker for the duration of your test run."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"using FluentAssertions;
using Testcontainers.PostgreSql;
using Xunit;

public class DatabaseIntegrationTests : IAsyncLifetime
{
    private readonly PostgreSqlContainer _postgresContainer = new PostgreSqlBuilder()
        .WithImage("postgres:15-alpine")
        .Build();

    public async Task InitializeAsync()
    {
        await _postgresContainer.StartAsync();
    }

    public async Task DisposeAsync()
    {
        await _postgresContainer.DisposeAsync();
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task CanConnectToDatabase_AndExecuteQuery()
    {
        // Arrange
        var connectionString = _postgresContainer.GetConnectionString();

        // Act
        await using var connection = new Npgsql.NpgsqlConnection(connectionString);
        await connection.OpenAsync();

        // Assert
        connection.State.Should().Be(System.Data.ConnectionState.Open);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for .NET Testing"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep unit tests completely isolated from file systems, networks, and databases by heavily utilizing Moq."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use FluentAssertions for readable test assertions that yield descriptive failure messages."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage Testcontainers IAsyncLifetime to manage container lifecycles cleanly without relying on pre-existing host environments."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Group related tests logically using xUnit classes and traits to easily filter test runs in CI/CD pipelines."}]
